            <ArticleTitle>p63 in Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Skin: More Than a Stem Cell/Progenitor Marker.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>280-281</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-202X(16)32619-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jid.2016.10.032</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The p63 gene is often overexpressed in squamous cell carcinomas; however, how its overexpression contributes to tumor formation and expansion is still incompletely understood. Devos et al. report the development of a versatile mouse model demonstrating that p63 facilitates squamous cell carcinoma formation in skin and providing an excellent tool to dissect the relevance of its downstream signaling pathways in tumorigenesis.</AbstractText>
